+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls**
+*   **Blotchy Surfaces:** Without identity regularization ($L_{ID}$), Gaussian primitives tend to adjust their colors instead of the appearance model, causing blotchy homogeneous surfaces [S1].
+*   **Unpleasant Colors:** Lack of regularization can lead to unpleasant colors in the reconstructed scene [S1].
+*   **Performance:** Querying the appearance model for every pixel is time-consuming; the cell-based query is necessary to minimize optimization time [S1].
+*   **Depth Accuracy:** The method relies on mean depth calculated during differentiable rasterization for back-projection [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The cell-based query reduces optimization time by computing transformation matrices for cells rather than every pixel. The number of queries is $(H/s) \times (W/s)$ instead of $H \times W$ [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing multi-resolution hash grids and the MLP parameters. The appearance module is discarded after optimization, so it does not impact rendering memory or speed [S1].
-*   **Performance:** Achieves state-of-the-art rendering quality with minimal training time and memory usage, without compromising rendering speeds [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Plug-and-play, decouples global and local appearance variations, ensures 3D consistency, prevents floaters and color distortions [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires differentiable rasterization to render depth. Performance depends on the quality of the hash grid and MLP [S1].
-*   **When to use:** Scenes with significant appearance variations (global and local) where coupled methods are too slow and simple decoupled methods fail [S1].
-*   **When not to use:** Scenes with no appearance variations, or where rendering speed is critical and the overhead of the appearance module during training is unacceptable (though it is discarded after training) [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Optimization time is reduced by using cell-based query instead of per-pixel query [S1]. Rendering speed is not compromised as the appearance module is discarded after optimization [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Minimal memory overhead due to the lightweight MLP and sparse hash grids [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Plug-and-play, decouples global and local appearance variations, ensures 3D consistency, fast optimization, no rendering speed penalty [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires differentiable rasterization with depth output [S1]. Performance on scenes with extreme local changes may depend on hash grid resolution [S1].
+*   **When to Use:** Scenes with global appearance variations (daylight, ISPs) and local changes (street lamps) where floaters and color distortions are present [S1].
+*   **When Not to Use:** Scenes with no appearance variations where standard 3DGS is sufficient, or when real-time training speed is critical and cell-based query is still too slow (though this is rare) [S1].
 
 ## Background
-DAVIGS (Decoupling Appearance Variations In GS) was proposed by Dahmani et al. in 2024 [S1]. It addresses the limitations of existing Gaussian Splatting appearance modeling methods, which are either tightly coupled with the rendering process (hindering real-time rendering) or only account for mild global variations (performing poorly in scenes with local light changes) [S1]. The method builds on 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) and incorporates multi-resolution hash grids (from NeRF) and a lightweight MLP [S1].
